1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:14,000
[Howard K. Smith] Good evening. The television and radio stations of the United States and their affiliated stations are proud to provide facilities for a discussion of issues

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
[Howard K. Smith] in the current political campaign by the two major candidates for the presidency.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,000
[Howard K. Smith] The candidates need no introduction. The Republican candidate, Vice President Richard M. Nixon, and the Democratic candidate, Senator John F. Kennedy.

4
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:39,000
[Howard K. Smith] According to rules set by the candidates themselves, each man shall make an opening statement of approximately eight minutes' duration

5
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:47,000
[Howard K. Smith] and a closing statement of approximately three minutes' duration. In between, the candidates will answer, or comment upon answers,

6
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:55,000
[Howard K. Smith] to questions put by a panel of correspondents. In this, the first discussion in a series of four joint appearances,

7
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:62,500
[Howard K. Smith] the subject matter, it has been agreed, will be restricted to internal or domestic American matters.

8
00:00:62,500 --> 00:00:68,000
[Howard K. Smith] And now for the first opening statement by Senator John F. Kennedy.

9
00:00:68,000 --> 00:00:76,000
[John F. Kennedy] Mr. Smith, Mr. Nixon. In the election of 1860, Abraham Lincoln said the question was whether this nation could exist half-slave or half-free.

10
00:00:76,000 --> 00:00:80,000
[John F. Kennedy] In the election of 1960, and with the world around us, the question is whether the world will exist half-slave or half-free.
